==Icelandic==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|non|is}} {{m|non|sitja}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|is}} {{m|gem-pro|*sitjaną}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|is}} {{m|ine-pro|*sed-||sit}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈsɪːtja/|lang=is}}
*: {{rhymes|ɪːtja|lang=is}}

===Verb===
{{is-verb-strong|sat|sátu|setið}}

# {{lb|is|intransitive}} to [[sit]]
#* [[w:en:Hávamál|Hávamál]] ([[s:en:Poetic Edda/Hávamál|English source]], [[s:is:Hávamál|Icelandic source]])
#*: ''Gefendur heilir!''
#*: ''Gestur er inn kominn!''
#*: ''hvar skal '''sitja''' sjá?''
#*: ''Mjög er bráður''
#*: ''sá er á bröndum skal''
#*: ''síns um freista frama.''
#*:: Hail to the giver! | a guest has come;
#*:: Where shall the stranger '''sit'''?
#*:: Swift shall he be who, | with swords shall try
#*:: The proof of his might to make.
# {{lb|is|intransitive}} to [[ride]]
#* Revelation 6-11 ([http://www.biblegateway.com/resources/readingplans/index.php/2010/12/29?plan=2&version=31 English] and [http://www.biblegateway.com/resources/readingplans/index.php/2010/12/29?plan=2&version=18 Icelandic])
#*: Og ég sá, og sjá: Bleikur hestur, og sá er á honum '''sat''', hann hét Dauði, og Hel var í för með honum. Þeim var gefið vald yfir fjórða hluta jarðarinnar, til þess að deyða með sverði, með hungri og drepsótt og láta menn farast fyrir villidýrum jarðarinnar.
#*:: I looked, and there before me was a pale horse! Its '''rider''' was named Death, and Hades was following close behind him. They were given power over a fourth of the earth to kill by sword, famine and plague, and by the wild beasts of the earth.

